Subject: [PATCH 10958/11171] Record that no cheap induce measurement exists:
 both probes are unusable

Searched for a bounded way to measure post-fix induction quality without a
multi-hour live eval, per the operator's "check for a cheap path first". There
is none, and the search itself is worth recording so it is not repeated.

arc_e3_induced_model_quality.py is offline and cheap but scores engines that
already exist, which is the opposite of measuring fresh post-fix inductions. Its
docstring carries a finding worth re-reading: induced models predict
near-identity, cell_recall 0 to 0.05 against an exact-match figure inflated by
no-op transitions, and the bottleneck is induction quality rather than the gate.

arc_generator_context_overflow_probe.py is the decisive context test. I ran it.
The control arm reported "server never became healthy" and the GPU never left 4
MiB, because resolve_gguf globs one hardcoded directory for
Qwen3.5-9B-MTP-GGUF, the retired generator. The live pin is Qwen3.8-27B. So it
measures the wrong model and on this host does not start.

That is the second harness found frozen on that retired pin in two weeks;
arc_scored_path_lever_harness.py is the first, and it behaves better by refusing
to start rather than failing as an unhealthy server.

Consequence: measuring post-fix engine emission and fresh-induction held-out
accuracy requires a live arc_leaderboard_eval run, hours rather than minutes.

Deliberately not repinned. The probe derives VRAM constants and its own
docstring warns a mislabelled arm propagates into a guard threshold, so pointing
it at a 27B model changes what its numbers mean and deserves its own measurement
pass rather than a one-line edit made during a search.

